{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T09:48:58Z","timestamp":1791798538405,"version":"4.3.4"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]]},"group-title":"Artificial Intelligence and Robotics","reference-count":0,"publisher":"California Digital Library (CDL)","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1791763200000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc-nd\/4.0\/legalcode"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":[],"accepted":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Achieving biodiversity targets requires advances in marine conservation planning and action. We make three arguments about large language models (LLMs): they add more value when embedded within decision-support systems; they integrate fragmented expertise, data, and tools; they can increase capacity in under-resourced settings through co-design. We illustrate these arguments with a conceptual LLM-enabled marine protected area design and management framework, and address concerns about data, governance, and knowledge equity.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.32942\/x2rt2g","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T09:20:07Z","timestamp":1791796807000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Large Language Models for Marine Protected Area Design and Management Planning"],"prefix":"10.32942","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4376-5567","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ysobel","family":"Sims","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1268-6137","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Joseph","family":"Maina","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author